{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1585589040/3/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4c3d4a41\" lines=\"15-28\">\n<description>\nFit a grey bar into a grey frame by aligning projections with colored columns, then stack colors above.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, a grey structural color forming two components: a large rectangular frame (slot) and a smaller bar with upward projections.\n- Colored pixels inside the slot mark target columns.\n\nRule:\n- Move the bar upward into the slot so its bottom edge sits one cell above the slot's interior bottom.\n- Shift it horizontally so the bar's upward projections align column-wise with the colored pixels in the slot.\n- Stack the colored pixels immediately above the bar projections in each column.\n\nKey insight: The bar's projections act as keys that must align with the colored-column \"locks\" in the slot.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"247ef758\" lines=\"35-46\">\n<description>\nMove colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.\n\nRule:\n- For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.\n- Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.\n\nKey insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"88bcf3b4\" lines=\"229-241\">\n<description>\nSwing an irregular line segment around a nearby obstacle sprite, preserving its pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, bar color (straight lines perpendicular to the grid edge, one end on the border), segment color (irregular stroke attached to bar's inner endpoint), obstacle color (sprite near the bar terminal).\n\nRule:\n- Each bar is perpendicular to the edge it touches; its inner (terminal) endpoint has an attached irregular segment.\n- The segment detaches and re-routes: it travels from the terminal toward the nearest obstacle, hugs the obstacle's boundary, then exits at a 45° diagonal.\n- Total painted pixels equal the original segment length.\n\nKey insight: The segment behaves like a rope swung around the obstacle — attach at the nearest point, trace the skin, exit diagonally, conserving length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"136b0064\" lines=\"25-39\">\n<description>\nDraw a polyline by decoding 3x3 glyph instructions read across a divided grid.\n\nInput structure:\n- Left of a full-height vertical divider: 3x3 colored glyphs on bg.\n- Right of divider: a single gray (5) start pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Remove the divider; shift the gray start into the left canvas.\n- Read glyphs in column-major order (left→right, top→bottom).\n- Each glyph's shape (pixel area) encodes a segment: area 4 → down 2, area 5 → right 3, area 6 → left 4, area 7 → left 2.\n- Append segments sequentially, stepping down one cell between each.\n\nKey insight: Glyph pixel count (area) uniquely determines both segment length and direction — the shape itself is the instruction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"d59b0160\" lines=\"39-54\">\n<description>\nDelete connected components whose colors match a legend swatch.\n\nInput structure:\n- Background (bg) fills most of the grid.\n- Top-left corner: a small square legend region bordered on its bottom and right edges by a single color; the interior holds a set of \"target\" colors.\n- Elsewhere: multicolored objects (4-connected components) on the bg.\n\nRule:\n- Read the target color set from inside the legend border.\n- For each connected component, check whether it contains **all** target colors.\n- Erase (fill with bg) every component that does; leave all others unchanged.\n- Output grid is the same size.\n\nKey insight: The legend acts as a filter specification — only components carrying the full color palette shown in the legend are removed.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2d0172a1\" lines=\"209-224\">\n<description>\nConvert a rough nested line drawing into a clean canonical frame-and-dot diagram.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.\n- Foreground 4-connected objects are rough closed frames or small filled blobs.\n- Frames may contain other frames/blobs; blobs outside a frame remain outside it.\n\nRule:\n- Detect the containment tree of foreground objects using holes of each frame object.\n- Replace every blob with one foreground pixel.\n- Replace every frame with a rectangular border padded two cells around its direct children.\n- Place siblings by their original row/column overlap and order, with one empty cell between canonical objects.\n\nKey insight: The exact wobbly geometry is irrelevant; only enclosure plus relative sibling positions define the output.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b80bb43\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nRemove diagonal staircase noise and complete broken straight lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background; one foreground color fg forming axis-aligned lines.\n- Some fg pixels form 45° diagonal chains (8-connected but 4-disconnected from lines).\n\nRule:\n- Delete all diagonal-only fg pixels (those with no orthogonal fg neighbor, only diagonal ones).\n- Fill short gaps in horizontal/vertical lines where a deleted diagonal was nearby.\n- Prune remaining diagonal-adjacent leaf pixels near former diagonal sites.\n\nKey insight: Diagonal pixels are hints marking where a straight line is broken; their span sets the maximum gap size to fill.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8698868d\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nMatch sprites to frames by connected-hole count, then nest each sprite inside its frame.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Large hollow squares (frames) arranged in a grid; smaller filled squares (sprites) placed elsewhere.\n- Each frame and sprite has a distinct color. Sprites may contain holes (non-sprite-color pixels).\n\nRule:\n- Frames form a regular grid; some may be bg-colored (invisible).\n- Count connected hole regions inside each frame's interior and each sprite.\n- Pair each frame with the sprite sharing the same hole count.\n- Place the matched sprite inside the frame border; recolor sprite holes to the frame's color.\n\nKey insight: The matching key is the number of connected hole components, not hole area or color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b0039139\" lines=\"29-44\">\n<description>\nRecolor a cropped template and repeat it according to a component-count stencil.\n\nInput structure:\n- Solid blue (1) separator rows or columns split the grid into four sections.\n- Section 1 is a black-background shape template; section 2 is a component-count stencil.\n- Sections 3 and 4 are solid color swatches for output foreground and background.\n\nRule:\n- Crop section 1 to the bbox of its non-black pixels and recolor it with the two swatches.\n- Count 4-connected non-black components in section 2.\n- Repeat the recolored crop that many times, inserting one background-colored row/column between copies.\n\nKey insight: The second section encodes only the repeat count; the output orientation\nis inherited from whether the input separators are columns or rows.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b10624e5\" lines=\"24-39\">\n<description>\nReplicate a motif across quadrants with scaling and reflection keyed to red anchor blocks.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into four quadrants by a full-row and full-column divider of a single non-bg color.\n- Each quadrant contains a red (2) square anchor block of varying size.\n- The top-left quadrant also has a colored motif attached to its red anchor.\n\nRule:\n- Use the top-left quadrant's motif as the source pattern.\n- For each other quadrant, scale the motif by the ratio of that quadrant's red block size to the source red block size.\n- Reflect horizontally for right-side quadrants, vertically for bottom quadrants (both for bottom-right).\n- Align the scaled/reflected motif so its red region overlaps the target anchor, then stamp non-bg pixels.\n\nKey insight: Red block size encodes the scale factor; quadrant position encodes the reflection axes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"898e7135\" lines=\"7-20\">\n<description>\nInflate a frame's hole-layout and fill each hole with its matching piece.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Largest component is a monochrome frame enclosing bg holes.\n- Outside pieces composed of k×k blocks; k = side of smallest solid square piece.\n\nRule:\n- Extract connected hole regions inside the frame as slots.\n- Match each piece to a slot by comparing block-level shapes under rotation/flip.\n- Output the layout scaled by k, with pieces placed in slots; frame color as background.\n\nKey insight: Pieces are k×k-block-scaled versions of holes; downsampling reveals shape matches under all 8 orientations.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3dc255db\" lines=\"37-53\">\n<description>\nEject content pixels from cup-shaped frames outward along each cup's tail direction.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Hollow cup/Y/funnel frames, each a unique border color.\n- Each cup has a convergent tail spike on one side and an open mouth on the opposite side.\n- Content pixels (a single non-border color per cup) sit inside the cup's mouth interior.\n\nRule:\n- For each cup, identify its tail spike and tail direction.\n- Erase all content pixels from the mouth interior.\n- Draw a straight line of content-colored pixels from the spike outward in the tail direction,\n  one pixel per erased content pixel; cells beyond the grid edge are dropped.\n\nKey insight: The tail spike is the unique convergent point whose outward direction is opposite\nthe mouth opening where the content lives.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
